## Loop discipline

Never grind on the same problem indefinitely:

- **Two strikes, change the approach.** If the same approach fails twice, try a genuinely
  different one. On the third failure, stop and report the blocker with its evidence (what you
  tried, what happened, what you need) instead of retrying.
- **Never game a gate.** Don't inflate a `Confidence: NN%` or mark a risk done just to get past a
  hook — an honest "stuck" is better than a false "done".
- **Bounded re-work.** Re-plan at most twice for one goal; resume a subagent whose output is marked
  partial (it hit its `maxTurns`) at most once, then escalate.
{%- if loop_guard_max %}
- **The loop guard.** A gate may block the same agent on the same thing at most
  {{ loop_guard_max }} times (fewer if its stated confidence stops rising). Then quality gates
  release it with the result marked **UNRESOLVED**, and approval gates stay closed and halt it.
  Every trip is logged to `.claude/loop-guard/escalations.md`. Before summarizing any work, read
  that file and report each entry as unresolved — never present released work as done.
{%- endif %}

## Naming what you refer back to

When a plan, report, note or draft lists things that something later refers back to — findings,
options, risks, success criteria, open questions — give each one a short kebab-case slug that says
what it is about (`cache-cold-after-deploy`, `version-the-keys`), and refer to it by that slug
everywhere: later in the same text, in other documents, and in later sessions. Never label them
with codes such as R1, A1, F2 or "option B": a reader has to look a code up, and the same code
means something else in the next document. Keep a slug once it is used; a list may still be
numbered for its order.

## Parallel exploration

Gather information concurrently, not step by step. When the next action does not depend on a
previous result, do it in parallel: batch multiple tool calls in a single turn, and for larger
investigations spawn several exploration agents at once — e.g. one fetching a web page, one
reading a PDF, one scanning the codebase. Exploration is read-only and side-effect-free, so
running it in parallel is safe and finishes faster; serialize only on genuine dependencies.
{%- if prefer_explorer %}

Delegate codebase research to the `explorer` subagent: the built-in `Explore` agent is turned off
in this project. Where instructions (plan mode's included) say Explore, use `explorer`. It has no
shell, so run git queries and GitHub requests yourself.
{%- endif %}

## Precedents

Before a change, research lists the precedents that bear on it: what the code already does that
the change must follow. Three kinds:
- **reuse** — an existing function, type, helper or test fixture that already does what the change
  needs, so the change calls it instead of writing it again;
- **pattern** — how nearby code already does the same kind of thing: naming, error handling,
  logging, configuration, tests, file layout;
- **contract** — what callers rely on: invariants, signatures, error types, file and protocol
  formats.

Give each entry a slug (**Naming what you refer back to**), its kind, its `file:line`, and what
the change must do: "call `parse_config` instead of reading the file", "return `Outcome::allow()`
on every early exit". List only entries that bear on this change, about ten at most; "None found"
is a valid answer. An entry is a fact the code shows, not advice. Departing from a precedent needs
a stated reason, approved like any other change to the plan.

