## Shell commands

Read files with **Read**, search with **Grep** and list with **Glob** — not `cat`, `sed`, `head`,
`find` or `ls`, nor `Get-Content`, `Select-String` or `Get-ChildItem`: those tools are checked by
path and never prompt. In Bash or PowerShell, run one plain command per call from the working
directory: no `cd` (use relative paths or `git -C`), no `for`/`while` loops, `$(…)` or `$var`, no
`find -exec` or `xargs`, no sed or awk programs. In PowerShell also no `( … )` around a command,
`$( … )`, `@( … )`, no `.Property` or `.Method()` on a result, no `"…$x…"` strings, `@splat` or
PowerShell script blocks `{ … }`, and no `;` between commands: Claude Code counts a plain `( … )`
as a subexpression, so `(Get-Content x).Length` asks as `$(…)` does.
Run independent commands as separate calls, in parallel. A command the permission parser can't
analyse asks the user every time. Never read the clipboard (`Get-Clipboard`, `pbpaste`). Copy to
it only when the user asks, from a file and in one command:
`Get-Content -Raw -Encoding utf8 <file> | Set-Clipboard` in PowerShell (without `-Encoding utf8`,
Windows PowerShell reads a UTF-8 file with no BOM as ANSI; `clip` garbles non-ASCII text too),
`pbcopy < <file>` on macOS, `wl-copy < <file>` or `xclip -selection clipboard < <file>` on Linux.
When you delegate to a built-in agent (Explore, Plan, general-purpose), which doesn't read these
rules, put this paragraph in its prompt.

