## Intents and issues

To turn a problem or idea into an agreed change, run `/intent <the problem>`: it improves the
prompt, investigates with evidence, and iterates on a plan until approved; then it can write a
numbered intent file under `{{ intent_dir }}/` (optional; the number checked against the remote main
branch so it is never reused) and drafts an actionable GitHub issue title and description. **Never
create GitHub issues** — the user files them (`gh issue create` is denied). Once filed,
`/intent #<number>` links the issue to the intent file, or to the issue draft when there is none;
`/deliver` can take either as its brief.
To review a change and record what the review finds the same way, run `/review-intent` (this branch
by default; or a pull request, a branch or a path): the project's agents review it in parallel, only
findings another agent confirms reach the issue draft, and it writes an ADR only when asked. It never
posts on a pull request.

**Every pull request and issue you draft needs the approvers' sign-off**, not only `/intent`'s.
Write its description with the Write tool to a draft: `.claude/intent/drafts/pr-<slug>.md` for a
pull request, `.claude/intent/drafts/issue-<slug>.md` for an issue (structured by
`.claude/intent/issue-template.md`), with a kebab-case `<slug>` no draft has yet; make sure
`.claude/intent/drafts/.gitignore` exists and holds `*`. End it with a **Needs from** section:
{% if approvers %}name the approvers in its heading (`## Needs from {{ approvers }}`), then one pending line
per approver, as @mentions (`- [ ] @handle — pending`) — GitHub notifies them through those
@mentions, and nobody is contacted any other way{% else %}"No approvers configured", and suggest
`ocgen edit intent --approver @handle`{% endif %}. The approvers can change while you work: after every
write, ocgen checks the draft against the project's current approvers and tells you who is missing —
its list wins, so fix the draft at once. Write each paragraph and each list item on one line (GitHub
shows every newline as a line break). Then tell me to type `draft` to review it in the browser, and
give me the command that files it as it stands:
`gh pr create --title "<title>" --body-file .claude/intent/drafts/pr-<slug>.md`, or
`gh issue create --title "<title>" --body-file .claude/intent/drafts/issue-<slug>.md`. Never open the
pull request or file the issue yourself.
